            <div type="bibliography" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="en">
                <p><bibl><ptr target="#Evangelidis1952"/>, <citedRange>302</citedRange></bibl> (<bibl><ptr target="#BE"/> <citedRange>1956, no. 143.8</citedRange></bibl>; <bibl><ptr target="#SEG"/> <citedRange>15.388</citedRange></bibl>); <bibl><ptr target="#IDodoneLhôte"/> <citedRange>36</citedRange></bibl> (<bibl><ptr target="#MéndezDosuna2008"/>, <citedRange>72</citedRange></bibl>; <bibl><ptr target="#NietoIzquierdo2019"/>, <citedRange>113</citedRange></bibl>); <bibl><ptr target="#IDodoneDVC"/> <citedRange>17B</citedRange></bibl> (<bibl><ptr target="#MéndezDosuna2018b"/>, <citedRange>287</citedRange></bibl>; <bibl><ptr target="#Johnston2021"/>, <citedRange>111, 112</citedRange></bibl>; <bibl><ptr target="#CIOD"/> <citedRange>15A+16B+17B</citedRange></bibl> [last consulted 10/07/2026]).</p>
                </div>
<div type="edition" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="grc">
    <ab>
        <lb n="1"/>αἰ κα μὰ ἔχ; 
    </ab>
</div>
            <div type="apparatus" xml:space="preserve">
    <app loc="l. 1">
        <note>According to <bibl><ptr target="#IDodoneDVC"/></bibl>, <foreign xml:lang="grc">ΜΑ</foreign> was first inscribed <foreign xml:lang="grc">ΜΕ</foreign>; <foreign xml:lang="grc">ΑΙΚΑΝ.ΑΕΧΟΝ</foreign>: <bibl><ptr target="#Evangelidis1952"/></bibl>; <foreign xml:lang="grc">αἰ κ’ ἄλ̣λ̣α̣ν̣ ἔχ</foreign>: <bibl><ptr target="#IDodoneLhôte"/></bibl>; <foreign xml:lang="grc">αἰ κα μὰ ἔχ</foreign>: <bibl><ptr target="#IDodoneDVC"/></bibl>; <foreign xml:lang="grc">ΑΙΚΑΜΑ̣Λ̣ ἔχ</foreign>: <bibl><ptr target="#CIOD"/></bibl>, for which Carbon conjectures <foreign xml:lang="grc">αἰ καμάν</foreign>.</note>
    </app>
</div>
  <div type="translation" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="en">
     <p>If I do not have?</p>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="es">
        <p>¿Si no tengo?</p>
        </div>
            <div type="commentary" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="en">
<p>According to Lhôte (<bibl><ptr target="#IDodoneLhôte"/></bibl>), this is a summary of Thelytos’ enquiry written by the personnel of the sanctuary in the local alphabet of Dodona. This hypothesis was rightly questioned by  <bibl><ptr target="#Johnston2021"/></bibl>, who, in turn, identified the text as the oracle’s response, although without further explanation (for the vexed question of the responses on the oracular tablets, see <bibl><ptr target="#MartínGonzález2021"/></bibl>). The different dialect, alphabet, letter forms, and phraseology used in this text, however, indicate that it is an independent enquiry, as argued by the editors of <bibl><ptr target="#IDodoneDVC"/></bibl>. If their reading is correct, the text consists of a concise question whose object remains unexpressed. The consultation takes the form of a conditional sentence introduced by αἰ (for this formulation in the oracular tablets, see <bibl><ptr target="#NietoIzquierdo2019"/></bibl>). On the negative <foreign xml:lang="grc">μά</foreign> for <foreign xml:lang="grc">μή</foreign>, see <bibl><ptr target="#MéndezDosuna2018b"/>, <citedRange>287</citedRange>.</bibl></p>
                 
                <p n="Ithaca results">In the first test, in which no space was included before the dashes used to pad the short text, Delphi ranks first with a probability of 29.54%, followed by Epirus (14.86%). Chronologically, the highest probability (4.87%) is assigned to the 450-440 BC interval, although the model distributes comparable probabilities across a broad range of dates. In the second test, in which a space was inserted before the dashes, Epirus ranks first (57.06%), while the probability assigned to the 450-440 BC interval increases substantially, from 4.87% to 14.34%. See the <ref target="https://github.com/IDodone/IDodoneOnline/tree/main/ithaca-results/IDodoneOnline17B">Ithaca results and associated files</ref>.</p>
                </div>
